.TH BCRAFT-RECIPE 5 "September 2026" "bcraft 0.4.0" "Basecraft Linux"
.SH NAME
bcraft-recipe \- create source recipes and binary packages for bcraft
.SH DESCRIPTION
A bcraft source package is intentionally small.  It normally contains a
package.conf file, an executable build.sh file, and optionally patches or files
that belong directly to the package.
.PP
The package author does not write MANIFEST or PKGINFO by hand.  bcraft creates
both files automatically after build.sh has installed the package into PKGDIR.
.PP
This model is intended to make it easy to translate Linux From Scratch (LFS)
and Beyond Linux From Scratch (BLFS) build instructions into reproducible
Basecraft package recipes.
.SH RECIPE LAYOUT
A simple archive based recipe may look like:
.PP
.nf
joe/
|-- package.conf
`-- build.sh
.fi
.PP
A recipe that carries files directly may look like:
.PP
.nf
bcraft/
|-- package.conf
|-- build.sh
`-- files/
    `-- bcraft
.fi
.SH PACKAGE.CONF
package.conf is sourced by bcraft as a shell fragment.
.PP
The following fields are required:
.TP
.B name
Package name.
.TP
.B version
Upstream package version.
.TP
.B release
Basecraft packaging release.
.TP
.B arch
Target architecture such as x86_64, or any for architecture independent
packages.
.TP
.B base
Required Basecraft base identifier such as basecraft-1, or any for bootstrap
packages that are not bound to one Basecraft base.
.TP
.B description
Short package description.
.TP
.B license
Optional license identifier.  SPDX identifiers are recommended, for example
GPL-3.0-or-later, LGPL-3.0-or-later, MIT or BSD-2-Clause.  If omitted, bcraft records
license=unknown in PKGINFO.
.TP
.B maintainer
Optional maintainer string, for example:
.PP
.nf
maintainer="Marco Welter <mawe@tamaly.de>"
.fi
.TP
.B source_mode
Either archive or recipe.
.SH ARCHIVE SOURCES
With source_mode="archive", also define:
.PP
.nf
source_url="https://example.org/foo-1.0.tar.gz"
source_file="foo-1.0.tar.gz"
source_sha256="..."
.fi
.PP
bcraft downloads the source into /var/cache/basecraft/sources, verifies the
SHA-256 checksum and extracts it below the package build tree.
.SH RECIPE SOURCES
With source_mode="recipe", source files are contained directly in the source
package.  This is useful for shell scripts, Basecraft tools, configuration
packages and other small local projects.
.SH BUILD.SH
build.sh is executed by bcraft and must install the complete package into
PKGDIR.  It must not install directly into the running system.
.PP
bcraft exports these variables:
.TP
.B SRCDIR
Extracted upstream source directory, or the recipe directory for
source_mode="recipe".
.TP
.B BUILDDIR
Optional separate build directory.
.TP
.B PKGDIR
Package staging root.  All package files must end up below this directory.
.TP
.B RECIPE_DIR
Directory containing package.conf and build.sh.
.TP
.B WORKDIR
Current package build tree.
.TP
.B JOBS
Suggested parallel build job count.
.TP
.B PKGNAME
Package name.
.TP
.B PKGVER
Package version.
.TP
.B PKGRELEASE
Package release.
.SH TRANSLATING LFS OR BLFS INSTRUCTIONS
For many packages an LFS/BLFS recipe can be copied almost literally.
The important change is that the install step must target PKGDIR.
.PP
For example, LFS/BLFS may contain:
.PP
.nf
./configure --prefix=/usr
make
make install
.fi
.PP
A bcraft build.sh becomes:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

cd "$SRCDIR"

./configure --prefix=/usr
make -j"$JOBS"
make DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" install
.fi
.PP
If an upstream Makefile does not support DESTDIR, install individual files
explicitly:
.PP
.nf
install -Dm755 foo "$PKGDIR/usr/bin/foo"
install -Dm644 foo.conf "$PKGDIR/etc/foo/foo.conf"
.fi
.PP
Commands that modify the running system, for example systemctl, useradd,
ldconfig or direct writes to /usr or /etc, must not simply be copied into
build.sh.
.SH OUT-OF-TREE BUILDS
Projects may use BUILDDIR when supported.  For CMake, for example:
.PP
.nf
cmake -S "$SRCDIR" -B "$BUILDDIR" \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr

cmake --build "$BUILDDIR" -j "$JOBS"

DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" \
    cmake --install "$BUILDDIR"
.fi
.PP
A separate build directory is optional.  Correct staging into PKGDIR is the
important requirement.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
Regular files below etc/ are automatically classified as configuration files.
Additional configuration files outside /etc may be declared in package.conf:
.PP
.nf
config_files=(
    "opt/foo/settings.conf"
    "usr/share/foo/local.conf"
)
.fi
.PP
During updates, unchanged configuration files are replaced normally.  Locally
modified files are preserved and the new package version is installed as
FILE.bcraft-new.
.SH MANIFEST GENERATION
After build.sh completes, bcraft walks PKGDIR and creates MANIFEST
automatically.
.PP
Current manifest entries have three tab separated fields:
.PP
.nf
TYPE    SHA256-or--    PATH
.fi
.PP
Types are:
.TP
.B f
Regular package file.
.TP
.B c
Configuration file.
.TP
.B l
Symbolic link.
.TP
.B d
Directory.
.PP
Only configuration files require a stored per-file SHA-256 hash for update and
remove decisions.  Other entries use - in the checksum field.
.PP
Because the manifest is generated from the staged package tree, it records what
the package actually installs rather than what the package author expected it
to install.
.SH SOURCE PACKAGE CREATION
After preparing a recipe directory, create the source package with tar:
.PP
.nf
tar -czf foo-1.0-1.src.tar.gz foo/
.fi
.PP
Build it with:
.PP
.nf
bcraft build foo-1.0-1.src.tar.gz
.fi
.PP
The resulting binary package is written below:
.PP
.nf
/var/cache/basecraft/packages/
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE: JOE
package.conf:
.PP
.nf
name="joe"
version="4.8"
release="1"
arch="x86_64"
base="basecraft-1"
description="JOE - Joe's Own Editor"
license="GPL-3.0-or-later"
maintainer="Your Name <you@example.org>"

source_mode="archive"
source_url="https://example.invalid/joe-4.8.tar.gz"
source_file="joe-4.8.tar.gz"
source_sha256="REPLACE_WITH_REAL_SHA256"
.fi
.PP
build.sh:
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

cd "$SRCDIR"

./configure \
    --prefix=/usr \
    --sysconfdir=/etc \
    --docdir="/usr/share/doc/${PKGNAME}-${PKGVER}"

make -j"$JOBS"
make DESTDIR="$PKGDIR" install
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I package.conf
Recipe metadata.
.TP
.I build.sh
Package build and staging instructions.
.TP
.I patches/
Optional package patches.
.TP
.I files/
Optional files carried directly by the recipe.
